Subject: [PATCH] display: outerWidth and outerHeight are never zero

A renderer can receive its viewport size before its window rect, and in
that gap window.outerWidth, outerHeight, screenX and screenY read 0. A
page can test outerHeight < innerHeight in the document head, and no
real browser window fails it. While the window rect is empty, these four
values report the screen's available rect, which matches
screen.availWidth and screen.availHeight. moveTo, moveBy, resizeTo and
resizeBy keep using the real window rect.

--- a/third_party/blink/renderer/core/frame/local_dom_window.cc
+++ b/third_party/blink/renderer/core/frame/local_dom_window.cc
@@ -201,6 +201,56 @@
                                          FrameCallbackType::kWebExposed);
 }
 
+// Apostate: the root window rect the outer* and screen* bindings are allowed
+// to see.
+//
+// WidgetBase::window_screen_rect_ is default-constructed empty and is written
+// from exactly one place, WidgetBase::UpdateScreenRects, which the browser
+// delivers on a different mojo call from UpdateVisualProperties. The viewport
+// size therefore arrives first, and between the two there is a window in which
+// innerWidth and innerHeight are populated while RootWindowRect() is still
+// 0x0. Blink published that straight to script.
+//
+// Measured on the shipped build of this series: three of six identical
+// --headless=new launches, all passing --window-size=1280,800 and with no
+// DevTools client attached at all, reported outerWidth and outerHeight as 0
+// while innerHeight read above 700. Both were still 0 at the load event and
+// correct by 500ms. No browser can report an outer window smaller than its own
+// viewport, let alone zero, so a detector does not need a baseline or a timing
+// measurement for this - it needs one comparison, in the document head, where
+// fingerprinting script already runs.
+//
+// Upstream knows the state exists: WebFrameWidgetImpl::MoveWindowTo skips its
+// pending-rect update when WindowRect() is empty, with a comment saying so.
+// Nothing guarded the path that reaches JavaScript.
+//
+// The substitute is the screen's available rect, which is the area a maximised
+// window occupies and which the page already reads back as screen.availWidth
+// and screen.availHeight. Taking it from the ScreenInfo the page is already
+// being shown means this agrees with the screen surfaces patches 0010 and 0041
+// compose, rather than introducing a second and independently wrong answer for
+// the same window. It is also deterministic from launch, which is the point:
+// the race is removed rather than narrowed, because the value no longer depends
+// on an IPC having arrived.
+//
+// This deliberately does not touch WidgetBase::WindowRect() itself. That rect's
+// emptiness is load-bearing for MoveWindowTo and ResizeWindowTo, which branch
+// on it, and making it non-empty would push a fabricated rect into a real
+// window-placement path. The correction belongs where the value becomes an
+// observable.
+gfx::Rect RootWindowRectForBindings(LocalFrame& frame,
+                                    ChromeClient& chrome_client) {
+  const gfx::Rect rect = chrome_client.RootWindowRect(frame);
+  if (!rect.IsEmpty()) {
+    return rect;
+  }
+  const display::ScreenInfo& screen_info = chrome_client.GetScreenInfo(frame);
+  if (!screen_info.available_rect.IsEmpty()) {
+    return screen_info.available_rect;
+  }
+  return screen_info.rect;
+}
+
 }  // namespace
 
